"Because Article V sets a very high bar, formal change is rare. An amendment normally needs a two-thirds vote in both houses of Congress and then the approval of three fourths of the states. Of the roughly twelve thousand amendments proposed in Congress since 1789, only thirty-three have been sent to the states, and twenty-seven have been ratified. Most of the work of applying the text to new situations has therefore been done through interpretation, as courts, Congress, and presidents apply eighteenth-century phrases such as “Commerce … among the several States” to railroads, radio, and the internet. How far interpretation may go is one of the oldest arguments in American law. It is also the reason a document of a few pages can still govern a country of more than 330 million people (2020 census).": "fb0a211a207e",
