"In May 1787, delegates began to gather in Philadelphia. Twelve states sent them; Rhode Island refused. They had been called together to revise the Articles. Meeting in secret through the summer, they ended up drafting an entirely new plan of government. Fifty-five delegates took part, and thirty-nine signed the finished document on September 17, 1787. The plan was built on compromises. Large and small states disagreed about representation, so the two chambers of Congress were filled in different ways: a House of Representatives based on population and a Senate in which every state has two seats. The delegates also decided that the plan would take effect once nine states approved it, rather than all thirteen.": "26a354f60da3",
"In most of life, laws come from the government. A constitution works the other way around: it is the law that creates the government in the first place. The Constitution of the United States, written in 1787, opens with the words “We the People,” and that opening is a claim about where power comes from. Political thinkers call the idea popular sovereignty: the people are the source of the government's authority, and officials hold power only because the people have granted it. In this sense the Constitution is a written contract. It sets up the institutions of government, hands each of them certain powers, and marks limits that none of them may cross.": "6829870bd313",
"In most of life, laws come from the government. A constitution works the other way around: it is the law that creates the government in the first place. The Constitution of the United States, written in 1787, opens with the words “We the People,” and that opening is a claim about where power comes from. Political thinkers call the idea popular sovereignty: the people are the source of the government's authority, and officials hold power only because the people have granted it. In this sense the Constitution is a written contract. It sets up the institutions of government, hands each of them certain powers, and marks limits that none of them may cross. The Constitution was the country's second attempt. After declaring independence in 1776, the thirteen states joined together under the Articles of Confederation, which took effect in 1781 and described the union as “a firm league of friendship.” Each state kept its sovereignty and had one vote in a Congress with a single chamber. That Congress could not collect taxes; it could only ask the states for money. It could not regulate trade between the states, and there was no separate president or national court system to carry out its decisions. Changing the Articles required every state legislature to agree, so their weaknesses were nearly impossible to repair. In May 1787, delegates began to gather in Philadelphia. Twelve states sent them; Rhode Island refused. They had been called together to revise the Articles. Meeting in secret through the summer, they ended up drafting an entirely new plan of government. Fifty-five delegates took part, and thirty-nine signed the finished document on September 17, 1787. The plan was built on compromises. Large and small states disagreed about representation, so the two chambers of Congress were filled in different ways: a House of Representatives based on population and a Senate in which every state has two seats. The delegates also decided that the plan would take effect once nine states approved it, rather than all thirteen. The document itself is brief. A one-sentence Preamble states its purposes, and seven articles follow. Article I creates Congress and lists its powers, such as taxing, regulating commerce, and declaring war. Article II creates the presidency, and Article III creates the Supreme Court and lets Congress set up lower courts. Article IV deals with relations among the states, Article V explains how to amend the Constitution, and Article VI declares the Constitution and the federal laws made under it “the supreme Law of the Land.” Article VII describes ratification, the formal approval that turned the proposal into law. Twenty-seven amendments have been added since. The original text, signatures included, runs to about 4,500 words. Several principles run through these few pages. The first is limited government: the national government has only the powers the Constitution gives it, which are called enumerated powers, and the Tenth Amendment adds that powers not delegated to it “are reserved to the States respectively, or to the people.” The second is the separation of powers among three branches, each able to check the others; that is the subject of the next lesson. The third is federalism, the division of authority between the national government and the states. Finally, the Supremacy Clause of Article VI settles what happens when the two collide: valid federal law prevails, and judges in every state are bound by it. Ratification was far from certain. Supporters of the Constitution, known as Federalists, argued that the union would not survive without a stronger national government; Alexander Hamilton, James Madison, and John Jay made the case in eighty-five newspaper essays now called The Federalist. Opponents, known as Anti-Federalists, warned that a distant national government would overwhelm the states, and they pointed out that the document contained no bill of rights. Massachusetts ratified only after the Federalists agreed to recommend amendments, and other states followed that example. New Hampshire became the ninth state to ratify in June 1788, and the Constitution took effect in March 1789. Congress then proposed twelve amendments, and ten were ratified in 1791. That Bill of Rights was in large part the product of a bargain. The contract also had deep flaws, and they were written into its text. “We the People” did not mean everyone. The Constitution never used the word “slavery,” yet several of its clauses protected the institution. In dividing up seats in the House, three fifths of a state's enslaved population was added to its free population, which gave slaveholding states more seats than their free population alone would have. Congress could not ban the importation of enslaved people before 1808, and people who escaped to another state had to be returned. Twenty-five of the delegates held people in slavery themselves. Voting rules were left to the states, which at the time shut out nearly all women and many others. Slavery was abolished only in 1865, by the Thirteenth Amendment, after a civil war. Because Article V sets a very high bar, formal change is rare. An amendment normally needs a two-thirds vote in both houses of Congress and then the approval of three fourths of the states. Of the roughly twelve thousand amendments proposed in Congress since 1789, only thirty-three have been sent to the states, and twenty-seven have been ratified. Most of the work of applying the text to new situations has therefore been done through interpretation, as courts, Congress, and presidents apply eighteenth-century phrases such as “Commerce … among the several States” to railroads, radio, and the internet. How far interpretation may go is one of the oldest arguments in American law. It is also the reason a document of a few pages can still govern a country of more than 330 million people (2020 census).": "e7468aa2bdce",
