"Ratification was far from certain. Supporters of the Constitution, known as Federalists, argued that the union would not survive without a stronger national government; Alexander Hamilton, James Madison, and John Jay made the case in eighty-five newspaper essays now called The Federalist. Opponents, known as Anti-Federalists, warned that a distant national government would overwhelm the states, and they pointed out that the document contained no bill of rights. Massachusetts ratified only after the Federalists agreed to recommend amendments, and other states followed that example. New Hampshire became the ninth state to ratify in June 1788, and the Constitution took effect in March 1789. Congress then proposed twelve amendments, and ten were ratified in 1791. That Bill of Rights was in large part the product of a bargain.": "e2ac89dc75e3",
"Representatives and direct Taxes shall be apportioned among the several States which may be included within this Union, according to their respective Numbers, which shall be determined by adding to the whole Number of free Persons, including those bound to Service for a Term of Years, and excluding Indians not taxed, three fifths of all other Persons.": "177916a6d800",
"Several principles run through these few pages. The first is limited government: the national government has only the powers the Constitution gives it, which are called enumerated powers, and the Tenth Amendment adds that powers not delegated to it “are reserved to the States respectively, or to the people.” The second is the separation of powers among three branches, each able to check the others; that is the subject of the next lesson. The third is federalism, the division of authority between the national government and the states. Finally, the Supremacy Clause of Article VI settles what happens when the two collide: valid federal law prevails, and judges in every state are bound by it.": "2dd8239b42b8",
