"The contract also had deep flaws, and they were written into its text. “We the People” did not mean everyone. The Constitution never used the word “slavery,” yet several of its clauses protected the institution. In dividing up seats in the House, three fifths of a state's enslaved population was added to its free population, which gave slaveholding states more seats than their free population alone would have. Congress could not ban the importation of enslaved people before 1808, and people who escaped to another state had to be returned. Twenty-five of the delegates held people in slavery themselves. Voting rules were left to the states, which at the time shut out nearly all women and many others. Slavery was abolished only in 1865, by the Thirteenth Amendment, after a civil war.": "37a51bd5b2cc",
